From 659c5f630ca3dd534eee97cc668a98710d461a6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 21:00:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] MFront: data shared by all the integration points
 (@SharedData)

The `@SharedData` keyword declares a data built once on the host by a C++
expression, for instance the slip systems of the grains of a polycrystal
read from a file. The behaviour accesses it through a constant reference
member. The `generic-parallel` interface copies the data in a memory
accessible by the device at the first call of a generated function (device
memory with the CUDA, HIP, SYCL and Kokkos backends, heap otherwise) and
gives its address to the kernels through the structure of the parameters.
Shared data are not supported in behaviour variables, which are built by
their default constructor in device code.

- `PolyCrystalsSlidingSystems` and `ExtendedPolyCrystalsSlidingSystems`
  store their data in fixed size arrays and are trivially copyable.
- The six polycrystal test behaviours declare their slip systems as shared
  data, compute their constants at each call instead of in function scope
  static variables, and report their errors with `tfel::fail` and
  `abortIntegration`. Their results on the host are unchanged.
- New comparison test: a Berveiller-Zaoui polycrystal with four grains.
- The test program of the `openmp-offload-nvhpc-gpu` configuration is
  linked with `libnvhpcmanaux`: `nvc++ -mp=gpu -gpu=mem:managed` does not
  link it, contrary to `-stdpar=gpu`, and memory allocated in managed mode
  was freed by the `free` function of the C library.
